### PAGE: Middleware

# Middleware

Middleware is a useful tool for logging, validation, and more.
You can easily register your own middleware using the `app.use` method.

Below is an example of a middleware that will log the elapse time of all handlers that come after it.

```typescript
app.use(async ({ log, next }) => {
  const startedAt = new Date();
  await next();
  log.debug(new Date().getTime() - startedAt.getTime());
});
```

---

### PAGE: Set up Agentic Users

# Set up Agentic Users

## 1. Set up an AI teammate

Follow [Set up an agent blueprint](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/registration) and select the AI teammate setup.

To run a config-free setup, provide an agent name:

```powershell
a365 setup all `
  --agent-name "MyAgent" `
  --aiteammate
```

## 2. Configure Teams SDK

Configure Teams SDK with the blueprint identity. It uses the same authentication settings as a standard Teams app, and the blueprint ID is the application (client) ID.

| Agent 365 value      | App authentication setting                                                       |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Blueprint ID         | Application (client) ID (`CLIENT_ID`)                                            |
| Blueprint credential | Client secret, user-assigned managed identity, or federated identity credentials |
| Tenant ID            | Tenant ID (`TENANT_ID`)                                                          |

Configure these values as described in App Authentication.

## 3. Deploy and register the messaging endpoint

Deploy your Teams SDK app to a publicly accessible host, then [register its messaging endpoint](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/agent-messaging-endpoint), typically `https://<host>/api/messages`.

```powershell
a365 setup blueprint --endpoint-only `
  --messaging-endpoint "https://<host>/api/messages"
```

## 4. Publish and create an instance

1. Run `a365 publish` to create the agent package.
2. Follow [Publish the agent](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/publish) to upload the package to the Microsoft 365 admin center.
3. [Create an agent instance](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/create-instance).

After the instance is approved, its Agentic User can communicate through the deployed Teams SDK app.

---

### PAGE: Static Pages

# Static Pages

The `App` class lets you host web apps in the agent. This is useful for an efficient inner loop — you can build, deploy, and sideload both an agent and a Tab app inside Teams in a single step. It's also useful in production, since it makes it straightforward to host a simple experience such as an agent configuration page or a Dialog.

To host a static tab web app, call the `app.tab()` function and provide an app name and a path to a folder containing an `index.html` file to be served up.

```typescript
app.tab('myApp', path.resolve('dist/client'));
```

This registers a route that is hosted at `http://localhost:PORT/tabs/myApp` or `https://BOT_DOMAIN/tabs/myApp`.

## Additional resources

- For more details about Tab apps, see the [Tabs](#tabs) in-depth guide.
- For an example of hosting a Dialog, see the [Creating Dialogs](#creating-dialogs) in-depth guide.

---

### PAGE: Agentic Identity

# Agentic Identity

:::note Availability
Agentic Identity support is available in Teams SDK v2.1 for TypeScript.
:::

These guides show how to connect a Teams SDK app to an **Agentic User**—an AI teammate represented by its own Microsoft Entra user account.

## Identity model

An Agentic Blueprint defines the shared credentials and baseline permissions for a type of agent. One blueprint can be used to create multiple Agentic User instances.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  B[Agentic Blueprint ID<br/>shared credentials and permissions]
  B --> U1[Agentic User instance A]
  B --> U2[Agentic User instance B]
```

One blueprint can support multiple independently managed Agentic Users while the same Teams SDK app handles their messages.

For background on blueprints, agent identities, and authentication modes, see [Agent 365 identity](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/developer/identity?view=o365-worldwide).

The guides show how to:

- Receive messages addressed to an Agentic User.
- Reply with the correct Agentic User identity.
- Send proactive messages with an explicit agent identity.
- Handle Agentic User lifecycle events.
- Add Agent 365 identity and correlation context to telemetry.

---

### PAGE: Dialogs

# Dialogs

Dialogs are a helpful paradigm in Teams which improve interactions between your agent and users. When dialogs are **invoked**, they pop open a window for a user in the Teams client. The content of the dialog can be supplied by the agent application.

:::note
In Teams client v1, dialogs were called task modules. They may occasionaly be used synonymously.
:::

## Key benefits

1. Dialogs pop open for a user in the Teams client. This means in group-settings, dialog actions are not visible to other users in the channel, reducing clutter.

2. Interactions like filling out complex forms, or multi-step forms where each step depends on the previous step are excellent use cases for dialogs.

3. The content for the dialog can be hard-coded in, or fetched at runtime. This makes them extremely flexible and powerful.

## Resources

- [Task Modules](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/platform/task-modules-and-cards/what-are-task-modules)
- [Invoking Task Modules](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/platform/task-modules-and-cards/task-modules/invoking-task-modules)

